==English==

===Etymology===
Likely akin to M.Du., M.L.G., E.Fris. ''tateren'' - "to chatter, babble", possibly of imitative origin. Attested in 1481, in William Caxton's translation of "[[w:Reynard|The History of Reynard the Fox]]" in the sense "to stutter", probably borrowed from [[w:Middle Dutch|Middle Dutch]].

===Verb===
{{en-verb|tattles|tattling|tattled|tattled}}

# {{senseid|en|to report others' wrongdoings or violations}}{{context|intransitive|pejorative|lang=en}} To [[report]] others' [[wrongdoing]]s or [[violations]]; to [[tell on]] somebody; to [[gossip]] or to disclose [[incriminating]] information.
# {{context|intransitive|lang=en}} To [[chatter]].
#* '''1599''', [[w:William Shakespeare|William Shakespeare]], ''[[w:Much Ado About Nothing|Much Ado About Nothing]]'', [[s:Much Ado About Nothing (Shakespeare)|Act 2 Scene 1]]
#*: BEATRICE. He were an excellent man that were made just in the mid-way between him and Benedick: the one is too like an image, and says nothing; and the other too like my lady's eldest son, evermore '''tattling'''.
#* Dryden
#*: the '''tattling''' quality of age, which is always narrative

